# boundarykit project policy

Architecture overview: [ARCHITECTURE.md](../docs/ARCHITECTURE.md).
Agent index: [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md).

When writing or reviewing Python, use:

- `.agents/skills/google-python-style-guide`
- `.agents/skills/python-solid-guide` (for OOP changes)
- `.agents/skills/boundarykit-domain` (assembly, exporters, fixtures)

## Tooling

- Package manager: **uv** (`uv.lock`; CI uses `uv sync --locked --group dev`)
- Format: **Black** + **isort** (Google profile, 80 columns) via `pyproject.toml`
- Lint: **pylint** with `.pylintrc` — **never** `# pylint: disable`; fix the issue
- Tests: **unittest** (not pytest)
- Python: `>=3.11` (`.python-version` is `3.11`)
- Pin exact versions in `dependency-groups` when adding or changing tools

## Conventions

- Absolute imports only: `from boundarykit import models` (no relative imports)
- Prefer Google-style docstrings
- Dependency injection: `RelationGeometryService` takes Protocols/concretes;
  `cli.build_service` wires them. Do not hard-code collaborators in the service.
- No new **runtime** dependencies without an explicit ask (stdlib-only today)

## Local verify (CI parity)

```bash
uv sync --locked --group dev
uv run black --check src tests
uv run isort --check-only src tests
uv run pylint --rcfile=.pylintrc src/boundarykit tests
uv run python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
```

Or run the project `/check` command.

## Git

- Do not commit unless the user asks.
- If asked for a commit message only, draft a conventional commit and stop
  (do not run `git commit`).
